{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gwh49s8KqXJXufnyiQjWwF","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/conclusion.tex:6:A joint empirical decision rule based on concentration and $t_1/t_5$ ratio correctly separates these cases across all five models, though the spread category rests on $N{=}1$ and requires further validation.\ndocs/paper/abstract.tex:6:We propose a joint empirical decision rule combining concentration and distribution shape ($t_1/t_5$ ratio) that correctly separates the beneficial case from the four non-beneficial ones across all five models studied.\ndocs/paper/introduction.tex:44:\\item We propose a joint empirical decision rule combining concentration and distribution shape ($t_1/t_5$ ratio) that correctly separates the one architecture where non-uniform allocation helps from the four where it does not, across all five models tested. Yi-1.5-9B serves as a critical test: its concentration exceeds Qwen's, yet its single-outlier spike pattern means MCKP provides no benefit over uniform (\\S\\ref{sec:heterogeneity})."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CtX3WQu52CTLhR8St6AXFu","session_id":"37274730-a89c-4c5a-ae32-bc24951806be","uuid":"fa01953e-8b58-4f5b-b5f2-16561ac3a450","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:03:07.738Z"}
