{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FBtwCPsXzwERKmeR9v8JEK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tdiff --git a/docs/paper/abstract.tex b/docs/paper/abstract.tex\n2\tindex 26e3464..cc7668c 100644\n3\t--- a/docs/paper/abstract.tex\n4\t+++ b/docs/paper/abstract.tex\n5\t@@ -1,8 +1,6 @@\n6\t-Per-head mixed-precision quantization of the KV cache promises lower perplexity than uniform quantization at the same average bit-width, but on hardware-aligned bit-widths $\\{2, 4, 8, 16\\}$, existing solvers fail catastrophically: unconstrained greedy and MCKP allocation achieve 32.45 and 36.92 perplexity on Qwen2.5-7B---far worse than uniform 4-bit (15.45).\n7\t-We trace this failure to \\emph{2-bit distortion compounding}: when the majority of heads are assigned 2-bit precision, per-head quantization errors interact non-additively through the residual stream, violating the additive objective assumed by standard solvers.\n8\t-We show that a single cardinality constraint capping the number of 2-bit assignments is a minimal sufficient fix.\n9\t-On Qwen2.5-7B at $4\\times$ compression, constrained allocation achieves 11.23 perplexity, a 27.3\\% reduction over uniform 4-bit.\n10\t-The constraint also renders solver choice irrelevant: constrained greedy and constrained MCKP produce equivalent bit-width assignments across all three tested architectures ($\\Delta{=}0.0000$), enabling use of a simple $O(H \\log H)$ greedy algorithm; per-layer brute-force oracle confirms near-optimality (max gap 0.02 PPL).\n11\t-However, the benefit depends on architecture: on Llama-3.1-8B the result is essentially indistinguishable from uniform (8.45 vs.\\ 8.47), and on Mistral-7B-v0.3, Yi-1.5-9B, and Gemma-2-9B non-uniform allocation provides no benefit.\n12\t-Notably, Yi-1.5-9B has the highest GQA ratio tested (8:1) and the highest top-5\\% concentration (0.71), yet gains nothing from non-uniform allocation---because its heterogeneity is a single-outlier ``spike'' with no cross-bitwidth sensitivity gradient, rather than the ``spread'' pattern that enables MCKP to redistribute budget across heads.\n13\t-We propose a joint diagnostic combining concentration and distribution shape ($t_1/t_5$ ratio) that correctly identifies all five architectures studied---distinguishing spread heterogeneity (MCKP-exploitable) from spike and flat patterns where non-uniform allocation provides no benefit.\n14\t+Non-uniform per-head KV cache quantization helps only when head sensitivity exhibits a specific distribution shape---a finding obscured by prior work's focus on aggregate metrics.\n15\t+We study five 7--9B architectures on hardware-aligned bit-widths $\\{2,4,8,16\\}$ and find that the benefit of per-head allocation depends not on GQA ratio or overall sensitivity magnitude, but on whether heterogeneity is \\emph{spread} across multiple heads with cross-bitwidth gradients or concentrated in a single outlier.\n16\t+On the one spread-pattern model (Qwen2.5-7B), a cardinality-constrained solver achieves 11.23 perplexity at $4\\times$ compression---a 27.3\\% reduction over uniform 4-bit (15.45)---while unconstrained solvers fail catastrophically (32--37 PPL) due to 2-bit distortion compounding through the residual stream.\n17\t+On the remaining four models, non-uniform allocation provides no meaningful benefit: Yi-1.5-9B has the highest concentration tested (0.71, exceeding Qwen's 0.61) yet gains nothing, because its heterogeneity is a single-outlier ``spike'' with no cross-bitwidth gradient.\n18\t+The cardinality constraint prevents catastrophic failure on spread architectures, moderate over-allocation on flat architectures (Gemma-2: unconstrained $1.47\\times$ degradation reduced to within 0.5\\% of uniform), and renders solver choice irrelevant---constrained greedy and MCKP produce identical allocations---enabling a simple $O(H\\log H)$ greedy algorithm.\n19\t+We propose a joint empirical decision rule combining concentration and distribution shape ($t_1/t_5$ ratio) that correctly separates the beneficial case from the four non-beneficial ones across all five models studied.\n20\tdiff --git a/docs/paper/analysis.tex b/docs/paper/analysis.tex\n21\tindex 3a52c48..c979c51 100644\n22\t--- a/docs/paper/analysis.tex\n23\t+++ b/docs/paper/analysis.tex\n24\t@@ -15,17 +15,12 @@ Qwen2.5-7B exemplifies the spread pattern (top-5\\% concentration 0.61, $t_1/t_5{\n25\t Constrained MCKP reduces degradation from $+$5.64 PPL (uniform) to $+$1.42 PPL, a 27.3\\% improvement over uniform 4-bit. %exp_id=qwen_constrained_mckp\n26\t \n27\t Yi-1.5-9B is a critical counterexample: despite the highest concentration tested ($0.71$, exceeding Qwen's $0.61$), constrained MCKP matches uniform ($+$0.20 vs.\\ $+$0.20). %exp_id=yi15_hires_constrained_mckp\n28\t-The mechanism is clear from the cross-bitwidth gradient structure.\n29\t-Yi-1.5's sensitivity is dominated by a single extreme outlier: Layer-4 Head-3 has a 2-bit delta of 13.12, while the second-ranked head has delta 0.55---a $24\\times$ gap ($t_1/t_5{=}0.90$, $t_1/t_2{=}23.7$). %exp_id=yi15_hires_profiling\n30\t-Critically, L4H3's sensitivity is confined to the 2-bit operating point: its 4-bit and 8-bit deltas are ${\\approx}\\,0$.\n31\t-The solver therefore has only one lever---upgrading L4H3 from 2-bit to 4-bit removes the distortion, but further upgrading to 8-bit provides no additional benefit, so the budget freed by downgrading other heads has nowhere useful to go.\n32\t-This is the ``spike'' pattern: high concentration driven by a single outlier with sensitivity at only one bitwidth.\n33\t+Yi-1.5's sensitivity is dominated by a single extreme outlier: Layer-4 Head-3 ($d_h(2){=}13.12$, second-ranked head: $0.55$, a $24\\times$ gap; $t_1/t_5{=}0.90$). %exp_id=yi15_hires_profiling\n34\t+Critically, L4H3's 4-bit and 8-bit deltas are ${\\approx}\\,0$, so upgrading it from 2-bit to 4-bit removes the distortion, but budget freed by downgrading other heads has nowhere useful to go---a ``spike'' pattern with sensitivity at only one bitwidth.\n35\t+In contrast, Qwen's Layer-0 heads exhibit non-zero deltas at multiple bitwidths (e.g., L0H0: 2-bit delta ${=}6.74$, with positive 4-bit and 8-bit deltas), giving the solver a multi-bitwidth gradient across several heads---a ``spread'' pattern enabling meaningful reallocation. %exp_id=qwen_hires_profiling\n36\t \n37\t-In contrast, Qwen's Layer-0 heads exhibit non-zero deltas at multiple bitwidths (e.g., L0H0: 2-bit delta ${=}6.74$, with positive 4-bit and 8-bit deltas), giving the solver a multi-bitwidth sensitivity gradient across several heads---a ``spread'' pattern that enables meaningful budget reallocation. %exp_id=qwen_hires_profiling\n38\t-\n39\t-The remaining three architectures fill out the diagnostic picture.\n40\t-Llama-3.1-8B shows above-threshold concentration ($0.57$) with a pronounced spike ($t_1/t_5{=}0.85$, $t_1/t_2{=}34.8$); its allocation is essentially indistinguishable from uniform ($+$0.36 vs.\\ $+$0.38). %exp_id=llama_hires_bf16_2bitonly %exp_id=llama_hires_constrained\n41\t-Mistral-7B-v0.3 ($t_1/t_5{=}0.41$) and Gemma-2-9B ($t_1/t_5{=}0.38$) both exhibit flat distributions with low concentration ($0.24$ and $0.23$ respectively), leaving the profiler with no reliable signal; constrained MCKP is slightly worse than uniform in both cases ($+$0.13 vs.\\ $+$0.10 for Mistral; $+$0.15 vs.\\ $+$0.13 for Gemma-2). %exp_id=mistral_c4_cross_dataset %exp_id=gemma2_full_pipeline\n42\t+Llama-3.1-8B shows above-threshold concentration ($0.57$) with a pronounced spike ($t_1/t_5{=}0.85$, $t_1/t_2{=}34.8$); its allocation is indistinguishable from uniform ($+$0.36 vs.\\ $+$0.38). %exp_id=llama_hires_bf16_2bitonly %exp_id=llama_hires_constrained\n43\t+Mistral-7B-v0.3 ($t_1/t_5{=}0.41$) and Gemma-2-9B ($t_1/t_5{=}0.38$) both exhibit flat distributions with low concentration ($0.24$ and $0.23$), leaving no reliable signal; constrained MCKP is slightly worse than uniform in both cases ($+$0.13 vs.\\ $+$0.10 for Mistral; $+$0.16 vs.\\ $+$0.14 for Gemma-2). %exp_id=mistral_c4_cross_dataset %exp_id=gemma2_full_pipeline\n44\t \n45\t \\begin{table}[t]\n46\t \\centering\n47\t@@ -41,53 +36,28 @@ Qwen2.5-7B & 7:1 & 0.61 & 0.57 & $+$5.64 & $+$1.42 & Spread \\\\\n48\t Yi-1.5-9B$^\\dagger$ & 8:1 & 0.71 & 0.90 & $+$0.20 & $+$0.20 & Spike \\\\\n49\t Llama-3.1-8B$^\\dagger$ & 4:1 & 0.57 & 0.85 & $+$0.38 & $+$0.36 & Spike \\\\\n50\t Mistral-7B-v0.3$^\\dagger$ & 4:1 & 0.24 & 0.41 & $+$0.10 & $+$0.13 & Flat \\\\\n51\t-Gemma-2-9B$^\\dagger$ & 2:1 & 0.23 & 0.38 & $+$0.13 & $+$0.15 & Flat \\\\\n52\t+Gemma-2-9B$^{*\\dagger}$ & 2:1 & 0.23 & 0.38 & $+$0.14 & $+$0.16 & Flat \\\\\n53\t \\bottomrule\n54\t \\end{tabular}\n55\t \\end{table}\n56\t \n57\t-\\begin{figure*}[t]\n58\t-\\centering\n59\t-\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/fig1_head_delta_heatmap.pdf}\n60\t-\\caption{Per-head 2-bit quantization sensitivity across five architectures. Qwen2.5-7B shows high concentration in Layer-0 heads (``spread'' pattern); Yi-1.5-9B shows a single extreme outlier at Layer-4 Head-3 (``spike'' pattern); Llama-3.1-8B shows a dominant outlier at Layer-2 Head-5 (49\\% of total distortion, ``spike'' pattern); Mistral-7B-v0.3 shows near-zero sensitivity across all heads; Gemma-2-9B exhibits the most uniform distribution, consistent with its low GQA ratio (2:1). Color intensity indicates PPL increase when the head is quantized to 2-bit.}\n61\t-\\label{fig:heatmap}\n62\t-\\end{figure*}\n63\t-\n64\t-Figure~\\ref{fig:heatmap} visualizes the per-head 2-bit distortion as a heatmap.\n65\t-The contrast is stark: Qwen's Layer-0 heads show high distortion (dark cells) while mid-to-deep layers are near-zero; Yi-1.5 shows a single bright spot at Layer-4 Head-3 (34\\% of total delta) with the remaining 191 heads near-uniform---a ``spike'' that MCKP cannot exploit; Gemma-2 exhibits the most uniform heatmap across its 336 heads, consistent with its 0.23 concentration; Mistral shows similarly low distortion across all layers and heads.\n66\t+The per-head heatmap (Figure~\\ref{fig:heatmap}, Appendix~\\ref{sec:heatmap_appendix}) confirms this visually: Qwen's Layer-0 heads show high distortion while mid-to-deep layers are near-zero (spread); Yi-1.5 shows a single bright spot at Layer-4 Head-3 (34\\% of total delta) with the remaining 191 heads near-uniform (spike); Gemma-2 and Mistral show uniformly low distortion.\n67\t \n68\t-\\subsection{Diagnostic Framework}\n69\t+\\subsection{Empirical Decision Rule}\n70\t \\label{sec:diagnostic}\n71\t \n72\t-The heterogeneity characterization shows that MCKP benefit requires not just high concentration but a specific \\emph{shape}---spread heterogeneity across multiple heads with cross-bitwidth sensitivity gradients.\n73\t-This motivates a joint diagnostic, based on five architectures, for deciding when non-uniform allocation is worthwhile:\n74\t-\n75\t-\\begin{enumerate}[nosep]\n76\t-\\item Run per-head profiling as described in \\S\\ref{sec:profiling}.\n77\t-\\item Compute the top-5\\% concentration and the $t_1/t_5$ ratio (top-1 distortion divided by top-5 distortion) from the resulting distortion table.\n78\t-\\item Select allocation strategy based on concentration \\emph{and} shape:\n79\t-  \\begin{itemize}[nosep]\n80\t-  \\item \\textbf{Spread} (concentration $\\gtrsim 0.50$, $t_1/t_5 \\lesssim 0.70$): non-uniform allocation recommended. Multiple heads exhibit cross-bitwidth sensitivity gradients, giving the solver room to redistribute budget. Qwen (0.61, $t_1/t_5{=}0.57$): 27.3\\% PPL improvement.\n81\t-  \\item \\textbf{Spike} ($t_1/t_5 \\gtrsim 0.70$, any concentration): use uniform. Sensitivity is dominated by a single outlier with no cross-bitwidth gradient; the solver cannot exploit it. Yi-1.5 (0.71, $t_1/t_5{=}0.90$) and Llama (0.57, $t_1/t_5{=}0.85$): no meaningful benefit.\n82\t-  \\item \\textbf{Flat} (concentration $\\lesssim 0.50$): use uniform. Near-uniform sensitivity provides no signal for differentiated allocation. When concentration falls below 0.50, the top-5\\% heads contribute too little cumulative delta for the solver to construct a meaningfully non-uniform allocation; consequently the $t_1/t_5$ ratio---regardless of its value---does not alter the outcome, and concentration alone suffices to determine the category. Mistral (0.24, $t_1/t_5{=}0.41$) and Gemma-2 (0.23, $t_1/t_5{=}0.38$): counterproductive.\n83\t-  \\end{itemize}\n84\t-\\end{enumerate}\n85\t-\n86\t-Llama-3.1-8B illustrates why both conditions are needed: its concentration (0.57) lies above the 0.50 threshold and would be misclassified as beneficial by concentration alone, but its spike shape ($t_1/t_5{=}0.85$) correctly predicts no meaningful benefit.\n87\t-Indeed, a concentration-only threshold would misclassify two of the three high-concentration models (Yi-1.5 at 0.71 and Llama at 0.57), making the $t_1/t_5$ shape condition essential for avoiding false positives.\n88\t-The joint condition---concentration $\\gtrsim 0.50$ and $t_1/t_5 \\lesssim 0.70$---correctly separates the beneficial case from the four non-beneficial cases across all five architectures studied.\n89\t-Both thresholds are illustrative, derived from five models; they should be validated on a broader set before being treated as general rules.\n90\t-The diagnostic adds no computational overhead: it reuses the profiling data already required for the allocation solver and avoids wasted optimization effort on architectures where non-uniform allocation provides no benefit.\n91\t-\n92\t-\\subsection{Granularity Tradeoff}\n93\t-\\label{sec:granularity}\n94\t-\n95\t-Quantization budget can in principle be allocated at three granularities, each with a distinct cost--benefit profile.\n96\t-\\emph{Layer-level} allocation (KVmix~\\cite{li2025kvmix}, KVTuner~\\cite{li2025kvtuner}) keeps the search space small ($L$ layers) but forces all heads in a layer to share one bitwidth.\n97\t-This is fundamentally limited when within-layer variance is large: in Qwen2.5-7B Layer-0, per-head 2-bit deltas are 6.74, 2.12, 1.31, and 1.24---a more than $5\\times$ spread that any layer-level assignment must average over.\n98\t-\\emph{Per-head} allocation (ours) exposes this heterogeneity to the solver and yields a 27.3\\% PPL improvement on Qwen, at the cost of $H$ profiling passes; the cardinality constraint developed in \\S\\ref{sec:cardinality} renders the choice of solver irrelevant, amortizing the search overhead.\n99\t-\\emph{Token-level} allocation (ZipCache~\\cite{he2024zipcache}, KIVI~\\cite{liu2024kivi}) is the finest grain available but introduces runtime overhead from online importance scoring.\n100\t-Importantly, per-head and per-token granularities are orthogonal: a head's bitwidth (static, determined offline) and a token's importance (dynamic, determined online) compose without conflict, so the two can be combined.\n101\t-Per-head therefore occupies the sweet spot for static allocation---fine enough to capture the head-level heterogeneity that drives MCKP benefit on spread architectures, yet free of any per-step runtime cost.\n102\t-\n103\t-Additional analyses---the effect of aggressive compression ratios, profiling resolution and multi-seed stability, per-layer optimality validation, and KV separation ablation---are provided in Appendices~\\ref{sec:aggressive}--\\ref{sec:kv_separation}.\n104\t+MCKP benefit requires not just high concentration but a specific \\emph{shape}---spread heterogeneity across multiple heads with cross-bitwidth sensitivity gradients.\n105\t+This motivates a joint empirical decision rule for deciding when non-uniform allocation is worthwhile.\n106\t+Given the profiling results from \\S\\ref{sec:profiling}, compute the top-5\\% concentration and $t_1/t_5$ ratio, then select:\n107\t+\\begin{itemize}[nosep]\n108\t+\\item \\textbf{Spread} (concentration $\\gtrsim 0.50$, $t_1/t_5 \\lesssim 0.70$): non-uniform recommended. Qwen (0.61, $t_1/t_5{=}0.57$): $-$27.3\\% PPL.\n109\t+\\item \\textbf{Spike} ($t_1/t_5 \\gtrsim 0.70$, any concentration): use uniform. A single outlier with no cross-bitwidth gradient cannot be exploited. Yi-1.5 (0.71, 0.90) and Llama (0.57, 0.85): no benefit.\n110\t+\\item \\textbf{Flat} (concentration $\\lesssim 0.50$): use uniform. Too little cumulative delta for meaningful reallocation; $t_1/t_5$ is irrelevant. Mistral (0.24) and Gemma-2 (0.23): counterproductive.\n111\t+\\end{itemize}\n112\t+\n113\t+Both conditions are needed: Llama's concentration (0.57) exceeds the 0.50 threshold but its spike shape ($t_1/t_5{=}0.85$) correctly predicts no benefit.\n114\t+A concentration-only threshold would misclassify two of three high-concentration models (Yi-1.5 at 0.71, Llama at 0.57).\n115\t+Only Qwen exhibits the spread pattern ($N{=}1$); the suggested thresholds should be treated as illustrative until confirmed on additional architectures.\n116\t+The rule reuses profiling data already required by the solver and adds no overhead.\n117\t+\n118\t+Additional analyses---granularity tradeoff (Appendix~\\ref{sec:granularity}), aggressive compression, profiling resolution and stability, per-layer optimality, and KV separation---are in Appendices~\\ref{sec:granularity}--\\ref{sec:kv_separation}.\n119\tdiff --git a/docs/paper/appendix.tex b/docs/paper/appendix.tex\n120\tindex 5604bff..88b063b 100644\n121\t--- a/docs/paper/appendix.tex\n122\t+++ b/docs/paper/appendix.tex\n123\t@@ -1,5 +1,104 @@\n124\t \\appendix\n125\t \n126\t+\\section{Compounding Curve}\n127\t+\\label{sec:compounding_appendix}\n128\t+\n129\t+\\begin{figure}[t]\n130\t+\\centering\n131\t+\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_compounding_curve.pdf}\n132\t+\\caption{PPL on Qwen2.5-7B ($4\\times$ compression) as a function of $k$, the maximum number of 2-bit heads. Too few 2-bit heads waste budget on 4-bit; too many trigger compounding. The minimum occurs at $k{=}8$.}\n133\t+\\label{fig:compounding}\n134\t+\\end{figure}\n135\t+\n136\t+\\section{Heuristic Baselines}\n137\t+\\label{sec:heuristic_appendix}\n138\t+\n139\t+\\begin{table}[t]\n140\t+\\centering\n141\t+\\small\n142\t+\\caption{Constrained MCKP vs.\\ heuristic baselines at 4$\\times$ compression. L0-random reports median and mean$\\pm$std over 5 seeds. All methods use per-model optimal $k$ ($k{=}8$ for Qwen, $k{=}2$ for Llama, $k{=}4$ for Mistral). Llama and Mistral use hires profiling.}\n143\t+\\label{tab:heuristic}\n144\t+\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n145\t+\\toprule\n146\t+Model & MCKP & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{L0-random} & Naive \\\\\n147\t+ & & Median & Mean$\\pm$Std & Positional \\\\\n148\t+\\midrule\n149\t+Qwen2.5-7B      & 11.23 & 11.52 & 11.69$\\pm$0.47 & 12.23 \\\\\n150\t+Llama-3.1-8B    & 8.45  & 8.62  & 8.62$\\pm$0.07  & 8.58 \\\\\n151\t+Mistral-7B-v0.3 & 7.16  & 7.20  & 7.24$\\pm$0.07  & 7.21 \\\\\n152\t+\\bottomrule\n153\t+\\end{tabular}\n154\t+\\end{table}\n155\t+\n156\t+On Qwen, MCKP (11.23) beats the L0-random median (11.52) by 0.29 PPL and the naive positional heuristic (12.23) by 1.00 PPL.\n157\t+On Llama ($k{=}2$), all methods cluster within 0.17 PPL; on Mistral ($k{=}4$), all non-uniform strategies are worse than uniform (7.13), with MCKP (7.16) closest.\n158\t+Heuristic baselines use a slightly higher budget (0.256 vs.\\ 0.248, a 3\\% difference); despite this advantage, they still underperform MCKP.\n159\t+\n160\t+\\section{Allocation Visualization}\n161\t+\\label{sec:allocation_appendix}\n162\t+\n163\t+\\begin{figure}[t]\n164\t+\\centering\n165\t+\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_allocation_comparison.pdf}\n166\t+\\caption{Bit-width allocation on Qwen2.5-7B at 4$\\times$ compression. Uniform assigns all heads to 4-bit. Constrained MCKP protects Layer-0 heads at 8-bit and assigns the 8 least-sensitive heads to 2-bit.}\n167\t+\\label{fig:allocation}\n168\t+\\end{figure}\n169\t+\n170\t+\\section{Per-Head Sensitivity Heatmap}\n171\t+\\label{sec:heatmap_appendix}\n172\t+\n173\t+\\begin{figure*}[t]\n174\t+\\centering\n175\t+\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/fig1_head_delta_heatmap.pdf}\n176\t+\\caption{Per-head 2-bit quantization sensitivity across five architectures. Qwen2.5-7B shows high concentration in Layer-0 heads (``spread'' pattern); Yi-1.5-9B shows a single extreme outlier at Layer-4 Head-3 (``spike'' pattern); Llama-3.1-8B shows a dominant outlier at Layer-2 Head-5 (49\\% of total distortion, ``spike'' pattern); Mistral-7B-v0.3 shows near-zero sensitivity across all heads; Gemma-2-9B exhibits the most uniform distribution, consistent with its low GQA ratio (2:1). Color intensity indicates PPL increase when the head is quantized to 2-bit.}\n177\t+\\label{fig:heatmap}\n178\t+\\end{figure*}\n179\t+\n180\t+\\section{Granularity Tradeoff}\n181\t+\\label{sec:granularity}\n182\t+\n183\t+Quantization budget can in principle be allocated at three granularities, each with a distinct cost--benefit profile.\n184\t+\\emph{Layer-level} allocation (KVmix~\\cite{li2025kvmix}, KVTuner~\\cite{li2025kvtuner}) keeps the search space small ($L$ layers) but forces all heads in a layer to share one bitwidth.\n185\t+This is fundamentally limited when within-layer variance is large: in Qwen2.5-7B Layer-0, per-head 2-bit deltas are 6.74, 2.12, 1.31, and 1.24---a more than $5\\times$ spread that any layer-level assignment must average over.\n186\t+\\emph{Per-head} allocation (ours) exposes this heterogeneity to the solver and yields a 27.3\\% PPL improvement on Qwen, at the cost of $H$ profiling passes; the cardinality constraint developed in \\S\\ref{sec:cardinality} renders the choice of solver irrelevant, amortizing the search overhead.\n187\t+\\emph{Token-level} allocation (ZipCache~\\cite{he2024zipcache}, KIVI~\\cite{liu2024kivi}) is the finest grain available but introduces runtime overhead from online importance scoring.\n188\t+Importantly, per-head and per-token granularities are orthogonal: a head's bitwidth (static, determined offline) and a token's importance (dynamic, determined online) compose without conflict, so the two can be combined.\n189\t+Per-head therefore occupies the sweet spot for static allocation---fine enough to capture the head-level heterogeneity that drives MCKP benefit on spread architectures, yet free of any per-step runtime cost.\n190\t+\n191\t+\\section{Budget Sweep}\n192\t+\\label{sec:budget_sweep}\n193\t+\n194\t+\\begin{table}[t]\n195\t+\\centering\n196\t+\\small\n197\t+\\caption{Effect of compression ratio on Qwen2.5-7B. Constrained MCKP provides meaningful gains at moderate-to-aggressive compression ($\\geq$4$\\times$). Standard-resolution profiling.}\n198\t+\\label{tab:budget_sweep}\n199\t+\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n200\t+\\toprule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UUjiyiRL2UdViTkN3ZVaBi","session_id":"37274730-a89c-4c5a-ae32-bc24951806be","uuid":"af8db69c-3ee8-4553-8997-f9ad12f9e708","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:07:36.392Z"}
